      en: "Makabana is a strategically located town in the Niari region of the Republic of the Congo, situated at a vital junction of the country's railway network. Historically, it served as the primary transshipment hub for manganese ore extracted from the Moanda mines in neighboring Gabon. Before the completion of the Trans-Gabon Railway in the 1980s, the ore was transported via a massive 76-kilometer cableway—the longest in the world at the time—from Gabon to Makabana, where it was loaded onto trains bound for the Atlantic port of Pointe-Noire. This logistical feat made Makabana a cornerstone of the regional economy and a symbol of mid-20th-century industrial cooperation. Today, while the cableway is no longer operational, the town remains an important administrative and logistics center for the forestry and mining sectors within the Niari Valley, maintaining its status as a key transport link.",
